{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "number": 44,
  "title": "Prop 44 \u2014 Clinic spending: what would a 90 percent rule do?",
  "fps": 30,
  "frames": 5126,
  "checked": "2026-10-19",
  "narration": "asset://4b47216f34b02fbbeb2bcf433317ed902433471cd2eb3480cf3fa3583eb05d7a.wav",
  "prompter_words": "words-global.json",
  "script": [
    {
      "name": "The big picture",
      "narration": "Proposition forty-four sets a spending rule for private nonprofit safety-net clinics. These clinics serve many low-income and uninsured patients. The proposal does not apply the same rule to every doctor, hospital, or publicly operated clinic. Covered clinics would have to spend at least ninety percent of annual revenue on health care services, leaving no more than ten percent for other expenses. The attorney general would define the categories in more detail."
    },
    {
      "name": "An illustrative example",
      "narration": "Imagine a clinic manager planning next year\u2019s budget. For a hypothetical clinic receiving ten million dollars, the ninety percent minimum is nine million dollars. If qualifying spending is eight million, the shortfall is one million. This is arithmetic to explain the rule, not a real clinic\u2019s budget. A nurse depends on both care resources and working systems."
    },
    {
      "name": "How it would work",
      "narration": "A clinic falling short would owe a penalty equal to the shortfall. It could recover that money by complying within five years. Temporary waivers would be available in some circumstances. The rule therefore has enforcement mechanisms as well as a target. Which costs qualify matters. An administrative label is not proof that an expense is wasteful; scheduling, records, and other systems can support care. Final definitions affect how clinics would change their budgets. The analyst estimates enforcement costs in the low tens of millions of dollars each year, funded by fees on affected clinics. Other public costs are uncertain and depend on how clinics respond, including whether some reduce operations or close."
    },
    {
      "name": "Why someone might vote yes",
      "narration": "A voter might want a larger share of clinic revenue devoted to services for patients, with reporting and consequences for falling short. They may believe a clear minimum is a useful accountability tool. Reaching a ratio, however, does not by itself measure quality or outcomes."
    },
    {
      "name": "Why someone might vote no",
      "narration": "You might ask whether one ratio fits different clinics. Another voter could worry that a rigid limit constrains necessary support costs, adds fees, or makes some clinics financially fragile. A clinic can improve its reported ratio in different ways; the important outcome is access to effective care, not only the percentage on a report."
    },
    {
      "name": "What polling indicates",
      "narration": "In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 34 percent said yes, 61 percent no, and 6 percent were unsure. These are dated survey results, not votes already cast."
    },
    {
      "name": "Make your own decision",
      "narration": "Yes adopts the new spending rule; no leaves existing reporting and spending requirements in place. Visit our website for the fuller explanation, source links, and official voter guide. Compare the tradeoffs, then make your own informed choice."
    }
  ],
